---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <div class="shell policy-page">
    <header><p class="eyebrow">The publication</p><h1>About</h1></header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>UR NOT MAD ENOUGH investigates decisions that affect people in the Yukon.</strong></p>
      <p>Public records are often technically available and practically invisible. A decision may be scattered across court judgments, audits, contracts, access-to-information releases, government reports and regulatory filings. Few people have the time to find those records, assemble them and test what they mean. We do that work.</p>
      <p>We do not start from the conclusion that an institution or person has done something wrong. We start with evidence. When the record establishes misconduct, failure, waste, deception or abuse of authority, we should say so clearly. When it does not, we should say that just as clearly.</p>
      <p>Our current focus is Yukon: public services, money, land, work and the decisions that shape life here. We question governments, political actors, companies, institutions and accepted practices. A consequential failure deserves scrutiny even when no law was broken.</p>
      <p>We are politically unaffiliated. We apply the same standards to everyone we cover, explain the basis for criticism and give credible contrary evidence its due.</p>
      <blockquote>The goal is not to tell readers what to be angry about. It is to show them enough evidence to decide for themselves.</blockquote>
      <h2>Three ways to follow the record</h2>
      <p><strong>Dossiers</strong> assemble deep investigations that journalists, officials and readers can cite. <strong>Briefs</strong> explain one substantially established finding without unnecessary length. <strong>The Ledger</strong> returns to published work and records what changed, what did not and what remains unknown.</p>
      <p>The formats differ in scope, never in reliability. We aim to make the consequence clear at the beginning, ask people with direct knowledge what the records leave unanswered, and show readers how to check the account.</p>
      <h2>Accountable by design</h2>
      <p>Our methods, source links, corrections and published revision history are meant to be inspectable. Evidence is cited at the point of use, and publication-safe dossiers may document how central claims were tested. Private sources, restricted records and personal contact details are never made public merely for transparency's sake.</p>
      <h2>Contact the editor</h2>
      <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${siteConfig.editorialEmail}`}>{siteConfig.editorialEmail}</a> for reporting leads, correction requests, republication permission or other editorial matters. Start the subject with <strong>Tip —</strong>, <strong>Correction —</strong> or <strong>Permission —</strong> so the sole newsroom inbox can route it promptly.</p>
      <p>Ordinary email is not a secure or anonymous tip channel. Do not send highly sensitive information until a suitable secure route is available.</p>
      <h2>Technology and privacy</h2>
      <p>This is a static website with no advertising, analytics, tracking pixels, account system or client-side application code. Following a source, petition or government link takes you to a third party with its own privacy practices.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
